      "tool_uses": [
        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_0193xSYMTXYeGcdg7qTbJMaL",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/single-composition-point.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Single Composition Point\\ndescription: Centralizing credential and path configuration at the Hub\u0027s startup isolates both secrets and file-system logic from the broader codebase.\\ntimestamp: 2026-11-28T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Single-Composition-Point\\n  - tech/dotnet\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both source pages are explicit architectural decisions; the pattern connecting them is direct and unambiguous.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2027-02-28\\n---\\n\\nBoth [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] embody the same architectural principle: resolve critical configuration once at the Hub\u0027s startup (composition point), then enforce that resolved state downstream rather than letting individual components re-discover or re-negotiate these values.\\n\\n**Credential scoping** centralizes secret management: the Hub\u0027s startup injects the Anthropic API key directly into the child process\u0027s isolated environment, never into the Hub\u0027s own process space. This prevents a compromised Hub from leaking the credential.\\n\\n**Runtime paths** centralizes file-system configuration: the Hub\u0027s startup resolves every required path in exactly one place, reading from configuration sources in priority order, validating them, and making the resolved paths available to all downstream code. This prevents path logic from scattering across the codebase.\\n\\nThe shared effect is that the Hub\u0027s composition point becomes the sole gatekeeper for both *what the system can access* (paths) and *with what credentials it accesses them* (scoped, ephemeral). This makes the system\u0027s security and configuration surfaces auditable and controllable in one place.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
      "assistant_text": "Now I can see a genuine synthesis here. Both pages describe architectural decisions about isolating and centralizing different aspects of runtime configuration \u2014 but the connection between them isn\u0027t explicit in either page. Let me draft an answer and then decide whether to preserve this insight.\n\n---\n\n## Answer\n\nThe two decisions follow a **parallel pattern of centralizing control at the composition point** \u2014 the Hub\u0027s startup \u2014 but they isolate different kinds of information:\n\n**[[credential-scoping]]** centralizes *secret credentials* at the Hub\u0027s startup. Rather than letting child processes inherit credentials from the Hub\u0027s own environment, the Hub reads the API key at startup and injects it only into the child process\u0027s isolated environment. This prevents a compromised Hub from leaking the credential even if the Hub\u0027s own process is inspected.\n\n**[[runtime-paths]]** centralizes *file-system configuration* at the same startup point. Every path the Hub touches is resolved in exactly one place at startup \u2014 reading from configuration sources in priority order, validating them, and making the resolved paths available downstream. This prevents path logic from scattering across the codebase.\n\n**The connection**: Both decisions apply the same architectural principle \u2014 *resolve critical configuration once at composition, then enforce that resolved state downstream* \u2014 to two different domains (secrets and locations). This ensures the Hub has a single source of truth for both what it can access (paths) and with what credentials it accesses them (scoped, ephemeral), making the system\u0027s security posture and configuration surfaces auditable in one place rather than scattered across multiple startup procedures.\n\n---\n\n## Synthesis Preservation\n\nThis connection is genuinely new to the wiki \u2014 neither page states it explicitly, and I had to reason across both to articulate it. This looks like a worth-preserving synthesis because it identifies a shared pattern that clarifies the architectural intent behind both decisions.",
